tasks.acceptance_prose:
GOAL: when the agent CLI reports what the attempt actually COST, agentflow records it and the cost report prefers it over any DECLARED estimate. Round 10 shipped declared bases (params_b / price_per_mtok_usd) because no provider reported a price; the transcript carries provider cost data, so where it is real it must reach the ledger.

1. `Usage` in src/transcript.rs gains `pub cost_micros: Option<u64>` — the provider's OWN reported cost for the attempt, in MICRO-USD, an integer so the ledger never carries float drift. In `usage_from`, read `usage.cost.total` (USD, may arrive as a float like 0.0123) and record `(total * 1_000_000.0).round() as u64` ONLY when it is finite and STRICTLY POSITIVE. A reported `0` means "not tracked" — every provider tested here reports exactly `0`, which is indistinguishable from a genuinely free run — so a zero is NOT a measurement: it is `None`, never `Some(0)`. Be defensive like the rest of the module: a missing `cost`, a `cost` that is not an object, a `total` that is a string/negative/NaN → `None`. The rendered log line appends the cost only when present: `usage: 4240 in, 2 out, 4242 total ($0.0123)` (4 decimals), and is byte-identical to today when absent.

2. `Receipt` in src/state.rs gains:
    /// The provider's OWN reported cost for this attempt, in MICRO-USD
    /// (integer, so the ledger carries no float drift). `None` means the
    /// provider reported no cost — which is NOT zero: every provider tested
    /// here reports exactly 0, indistinguishable from "not tracked", so a
    /// reported 0 is recorded as `None`, never as a measured free run.
    #[serde(default)]
    pub cost_micros: Option<u64>,
`Receipt` is built with struct literals in TEN files — src/execute.rs, src/run.rs, src/state.rs, src/router.rs, tests/budget.rs, tests/receipt_atomic.rs, tests/conformance.rs, tests/ledger_truth.rs, tests/interrupted.rs, tests/cli.rs — 33 sites in total, and EVERY ONE must compile (add `cost_micros: None,` to fixtures/helpers). Wire the real value at the receipt-construction site in src/execute.rs where `tokens` is set today, and keep the rule that already governs `tokens`: a FAILED attempt still records what it spent.

3. REPORT (the `cost` fn in src/run.rs, reusing the pure helpers). A measured cost is REAL MONEY and outranks a declared basis for the attempt it belongs to. Put the folding rule in src/cost.rs as a pure, unit-tested type, e.g.
    pub enum Expense { Usd { micros: u64, estimated: bool }, Ratio(f64) }
   * receipt with `cost_micros: Some(m)` → `Usd { micros: m, estimated: false }` (measured);
   * else a `Priced(p)` basis + tokens → `Usd { estimated: true }` (dollars = tokens x price);
   * else a `Sized(s)` basis → `Ratio(...)` (token-weighted across a row's attempts);
   * else nothing.
   The row rules keep round 10's invariant EXACTLY: a row is in DOLLARS only when every attempt in it can be expressed in dollars (measured or a declared price) — a measured dollar amount and a parameter RATIO are incommensurable, so a measured+sized row stays `-`, just as a price+sized row does today; a row is a RATIO only when every attempt is Sized. A dollars figure containing ANY estimated term is marked `~` (`~$0.0123`), so an assumption is never presented as a measurement; a row of purely measured costs carries no `~`. `-` still means unknown, never free. The `cost basis:` line names what was in force, e.g. `cost basis: provider-reported (USD); 1 of 2 attempt(s) estimated from a declared price`, and keeps the existing lines byte-for-byte when no receipt carries a measured cost. src/router.rs IS in your scope, but for ONE reason only: its test module builds a `Receipt` fixture, so it must gain `cost_micros: None` to compile. Change NOTHING else there — in particular do not touch `Router::pick`, the cost tie-break or the score logic: a measured cost is a per-attempt fact, not a worker property, and it must never influence routing.

4. TESTS — use these exact names:
   * tests/token_cost.rs `a_reported_cost_is_captured_and_a_zero_is_not`: with the stub reporting a known non-zero `cost.total`, the receipt's `cost_micros` equals that USD value in micro-USD and the log renders it; a second arm with `cost.total == 0` yields `cost_micros: None` with the log line unchanged.
   * tests/cli.rs `the_cost_report_prefers_a_measured_cost_over_a_declared_one`: a receipt carrying a measured cost on a worker that ALSO declares a price shows the measured dollars and NO `~`; a row mixing a measured attempt with an estimated-priced attempt shows summed dollars WITH `~`; a measured+sized mix stays `-`; the basis line names the measured source.
   To let the stub report a cost, extend src/bin/example_agent.rs: env `FAKE_AGENT_JSON_COST` (USD as a decimal string, e.g. "0.0123") makes `json_transcript_events` emit it as `cost.total`; the default stays 0. Keep that function pure and unit-tested, and keep the stub a thin printer: llvm-cov does NOT credit a line inside a spawned stub binary, so all real parsing must live in library code.

5. SPECS: openspec/specs/state/spec.md — the receipt requirement gains the measured-cost field and the "only when strictly positive" rule; openspec/specs/cli/spec.md — the cost-report requirement gains measured-beats-declared, the `~` marker and the basis-line wording. Reproduce every surviving scenario verbatim and keep `openspec validate --specs` at 7/7.

GOAL: round 9 records an attempt lost to a killed orchestrator as an `interrupted` receipt, but honestly writes `worker: "unknown"`, `wall_clock_s: 0.0` and "duration unknown" — because the task state persisted neither WHO was running nor WHEN the attempt started. Persist both at dispatch, so the heal can tell the truth instead of a placeholder.

1. `TaskStatus` in src/config.rs gains two fields, both `#[serde(default)]`, both `Option`, written at dispatch and consumed ONLY for a `Running` entry (nothing clears them, and nothing reads them for a finished task):
    /// Unix ts when the running attempt was dispatched. Persisted BEFORE the
    /// agent spawns, so a killed orchestrator still leaves behind the moment
    /// the attempt started — the only thing that makes its spend measurable.
    #[serde(default)]
    pub attempt_started_ts: Option<u64>,
    /// The worker that attempt was dispatched to: the task state is the only
    /// place a killed orchestrator's choice survives.
    #[serde(default)]
    pub attempt_worker: Option<String>,
`TaskStatus` is built with struct literals in ELEVEN files — src/run.rs, src/state.rs, src/scheduler.rs, tests/budget.rs, tests/journal.rs, tests/state_strict.rs, tests/state_race.rs, tests/contract_scheduler.rs, tests/conformance.rs, tests/interrupted.rs, tests/cli.rs — 23 sites, and EVERY ONE must compile (`attempt_started_ts: None, attempt_worker: None,` for fixtures).

2. DISPATCH: in src/run.rs, the block that sets `s.state = TaskState::Running` (around line 369) already persists with `store.save(&status)` BEFORE the agent is spawned. Set `s.attempt_started_ts = Some(crate::state::now_ts())` and `s.attempt_worker = Some(worker.name.clone())` in that same mutation, so a crash in the window is still recoverable — that ordering is the whole point of the change.

3. HEAL (`fn heal_stale_attempt` in src/run.rs; the `Receipt` around line 741 writes `worker: UNKNOWN_WORKER`, `model: UNKNOWN_WORKER`, `wall_clock_s: 0.0` today):
   * `worker` = the persisted `attempt_worker`, else `UNKNOWN_WORKER`;
   * `model` = the configured worker's model when that worker is still in the config, else `UNKNOWN_WORKER` (a healed attempt must never claim a model nobody recorded);
   * `wall_clock_s` = `now_ts() - attempt_started_ts` when present, else `0.0`;
   * `error` stays truthful about what is actually measured: with a start time, "orchestrator exited mid-attempt; duration is time since dispatch (the exit time is unknown, so this is an upper bound)"; without one, keep today's exact text "orchestrator exited mid-attempt; attempt duration unknown".
   The outcome stays `interrupted` and stays OUT of worker trust (`counts_as_verdict`): a measured duration does NOT turn it into a verdict, and the cost report must not footnote a now-named worker as missing.

4. TESTS — exact names, both in tests/interrupted.rs:
   * `the_healed_receipt_names_the_worker_and_an_upper_bound_duration`: seed a `Running` entry with both fields set, heal, then assert the receipt names that worker AND its configured model, carries a `wall_clock_s` at least the elapsed offset (not 0.0), says "upper bound", and still does not enter `WINS/TOTAL`.
   * `a_state_without_attempt_identity_heals_as_before`: a `Running` entry with neither field (an older state file, or a crash before the first save) yields `worker: unknown`, `wall_clock_s: 0.0` and the ORIGINAL error text — the pre-change behaviour exactly.

5. SPECS: openspec/specs/state/spec.md — the interrupted-attempt requirement gains what is persisted at dispatch and that the healed receipt names the worker and an upper-bound duration when they are known, while the unknown case keeps the placeholder. Reproduce every surviving scenario verbatim; keep `openspec validate --specs` at 7/7.

GOAL: a failed attempt's committed work must never be destroyed. Round 7 taught this for the GATE path (the branch is kept on purpose and the retry re-runs only the gate). Two paths still delete it: a SCOPE VIOLATION and a MERGE CONFLICT. Both happen AFTER the agent did its paid work, and both currently throw that work away and pay for it again.

1. src/worktree.rs gains, next to the existing `remove` / `remove_worktree_only`:
    /// Keep a COPY of `branch`'s tip under a fresh, discoverable name —
    /// `<branch>-rejected-<now>` — so the work an attempt paid for survives
    /// the cleanup that follows a failure, without disturbing the original
    /// branch (the retry MUST still start clean from the current base).
    /// Returns the new branch name. Never fails the caller: a git error or a
    /// name collision that cannot be resolved yields `None`, because the
    /// attempt's own failure is the story and archiving is a courtesy.
    pub fn archive_branch(repo: &Path, branch: &str, now: u64) -> Option<String>
   Implement it with `git branch <new> <branch>` (a COPY — never a rename, so the existing cleanup and the retry are unaffected) and, if that name already exists, retry with a `-<n>` suffix. Leave the worktree alone.

2. src/execute.rs: on the SCOPE VIOLATION path (the message "attempt edited files out of scope", around line 325) and on the MERGE-CONFLICT path (around line 396), archive the branch BEFORE the existing `cleanup(&repo, &wt)` and append the archived name to the failure message, e.g.
     attempt edited files out of scope: rogue.txt (allowed: DONE.txt); work kept on branch <name>
   so the receipt — and therefore `af api results` — names exactly what was preserved. When archiving yields `None` the message must be unchanged, with no empty suffix.
   Change NOTHING else: not the success path, not the round-7 gate-failure path (that branch is deliberately kept and replayed), not the pre-agent failure paths (nothing was committed), and not the retry/resume logic. The retry must still create a clean branch off the current base — preserving the work under a NEW name is precisely what makes that safe.

3. TESTS — exact names:
   * tests/scope_enforcement.rs `a_scope_violation_keeps_the_agents_work_on_an_archived_branch`: run an attempt whose agent commits a file outside the task's scope; assert the attempt failed, that the receipt's error names the archived branch, that `git branch --list '<branch>-rejected-*'` lists it, that the archived branch really contains the agent's commit (the work is recoverable), and that the original branch is gone (so the retry is clean).
   * tests/e2e.rs `a_merge_conflict_keeps_the_agents_work_on_an_archived_branch`: the same for a conflicting merge.
   Reuse each file's existing harness and fixtures.

4. SPECS: openspec/specs/worktree/spec.md gains a requirement (with scenarios for both triggers) that work from a rejected attempt is preserved under an archived branch name and that archiving can never fail the attempt. Reproduce every surviving scenario verbatim; keep `openspec validate --specs` at 7/7.
